   "id": "p2-12",
   "name": "The action of heat on materials"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Heat can change materials in several ways:\n• Expansion: solids, liquids and gases get bigger when heated (gases expand the most). Engineers leave gaps in bridges and railway lines; a tight metal lid opens after it is warmed.\n• Change of state: heat melts solids and boils liquids.\n• Change of colour or burning: paper and wood burn and change into ash, smoke and gases; bread turns brown when toasted.\n• Decomposition: some substances break down into new ones (sugar heated strongly turns black: carbon).\nSome changes can be reversed (melting ice, expansion); others cannot (burning, cooking an egg).\nA bimetallic strip (two metals joined) bends when heated because the metals expand by different amounts: it is used in thermostats in irons and fridges.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "m": "a tight metal lid"
  },
  "problem": "Explain why pouring hot water over {m} on a glass jar helps to open it.",
  "steps": [
   "The hot water heats the metal lid.",
   "Metal expands more than glass when heated, so the lid becomes slightly bigger and loosens."
  ],
  "answer": "The metal lid expands more than the glass, so it loosens.",
  "checks": []
 },
